<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Habibullah Kalakani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bacha-i Saqao, Habibullah Ghazi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Habibullah Kalakani, nicknamed Bacha-i Saqao ('son of the water carrier'), was a Tajik rebel from the Shomali plain north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. Exploiting the revolts against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a>'s reforms, he seized Kabul in January 1929 and ruled as emir for nine months. Nadir Khan's forces took Kabul in October 1929, and Habibullah was executed in November.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/habibullah-kalakani.jpg" alt="Habibullah Kalakani" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Habibull%C4%81h_Kalak%C4%81ni_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Habibullah Kalakani was born Habibullah Khan on 19 January 1891 in the village of Kalakan, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, and was an ethnic Tajik. His father was a water carrier for Afghan soldiers in Kabul during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a>. At fourteen he rode to Kabul with two friends. He later joined King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a>'s army and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-afghan-war">Third Anglo-Afghan War</a> and the Khost rebellion of 1924, serving with distinction as an officer of the Model Battalion. He deserted at some unspecified time, worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>, and was arrested at Parachinar and sentenced by the British to eleven months in prison.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1928, while the Afghan army was engaged with Pashtun tribes in Laghman and Nangarhar in the east, the Saqqawists led by Kalakani attacked the unprotected capital from the north. The revolt grew into the Afghan Civil War of 1928 and 1929. On the night of 14 January 1929 Amanullah handed the kingdom to his brother Inayatullah Khan and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. Two days later Kalakani demanded that Inayatullah surrender or fight, and Inayatullah agreed to abdicate. Kalakani was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 17 January to 13 October 1929, a period known in Afghan historiography as the Saqqawist period. No country recognised him as ruler.</p><p>The Pashtun tribes, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai</a>, chafed under a non-Pashtun ruler. Nadir Shah, who had been Amanullah's minister of war, and his Musahiban brothers raised a force mostly from the British side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durand-line">Durand Line</a>. Anti-Soviet Basmachi rebels, who had taken refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, also launched raids into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and were expelled from Afghanistan after his fall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nadir's force took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 13 October 1929. Nadir considered pardoning Kalakani, even swearing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> according to some accounts, but pressure from loyal tribes led him to execute Kalakani and some of his closest followers on 1 November 1929. His reported last words were that he had nothing to ask of God, who had made him king. His remains lay at an undisclosed location for 87 years. After a campaign in 2016 by some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a> and scholars, he was buried on the Shahrara hill on 2 September 2016, after clashes between his supporters and soldiers loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-rashid-dostum">Abdul Rashid Dostum</a> in which one person died and four were injured.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Habibullah Kalakani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Habibullah Kalakani</b>, a Tajik bandit leader from the Shomali plain north of Kabul, known as Bacha-i Saqao, 'son of the water carrier', captured Kabul in January 1929, and Amanullah abdicated and fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/saqqawist-emirate">Saqqawist Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.53,69.17&amp;year=1915" aria-label="Kabul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Habibullah Kalakani"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5643 4587h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5643 4587h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.53,69.17&amp;year=1915">What was here in 1915? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
